Acute Prostatitis
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Fever/chills, Exquisitely tender/boggy prostate, Severe perineal pain, Pyuria/bacteriuria

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-002
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of high fevers, rigors, and excruciating perineal pain causing acute urinary distress, demanding immediate assessment to rule out sepsis and acute urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapid onset of high fever, chills, and severe pelvic pain associated with difficulty passing urine. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically necessary to exclude acute urinary retention and early urosepsis, conditions that carry significant morbidity if evaluation is delayed until the subsequent in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm freezing but sweating through my clothes, and it feels like I'm sitting on a hot coal whenever I try to pee."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 24 hours ago with a sudden onset of high fever and severe chills. The patient reports the rapid development of intense, localized perineal pain radiating to the suprapubic and lower back regions. Over the last 12 hours, he has experienced severe dysuria characterized by a sharp, burning sensation, alongside profoundly increased urinary frequency and urgency. He reports significant difficulty initiating a urinary stream, describing the urine as cloudy and malodorous. He also notes a generalized myalgia, systemic malaise, and painful ejaculation occurring the night prior.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently mobilizing with severe antalgic gait due to pelvic pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; risk of unobserved deterioration if systemic sepsis ensues.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.9C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Acutely unwell, diaphoretic, shivering, and shifting uncomfortably in the chair due to perineal pressure.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Suprapubic tenderness on deep palpation, bladder not distended. Genitourinary: Exquisite tenderness of the prostate gland on gentle digital rectal examination, presenting as warm, boggy, and significantly swollen. Severe perineal pain elicited on palpation. Point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates dense pyuria and bacteriuria, strongly supporting the diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: House is well-kept; patient has placed a heating pad on the armchair. Thermostat is set very high due to patient's subjective chills.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has taken two doses of over-the-counter paracetamol with minimal antipyretic effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Prostatitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of costovertebral angle tenderness and the distinct presence of a boggy, tender prostate.

Acute Epididymo-orchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to normal, non-tender testicles and epididymides on scrotal examination.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of high fever, boggy prostate, and absence of colicky unilateral flank pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits classic symptoms of a severe acute genitourinary infection, including high fever, tachycardia, and localized exquisite prostatic pain. The risk of rapid progression to urosepsis or complete acute urinary retention secondary to prostatic inflammation is exceptionally high. Conducting a prompt after-hours clinical evaluation, including a digital rectal examination and urinalysis, was imperative to confirm the localized source of infection and initiate empirical antibiotic therapy immediately. Postponing this assessment to the next in-hours period would have drastically increased the likelihood of systemic haemodynamic compromise or bladder outlet obstruction requiring invasive emergency intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ceftriaxone 1g stat and commenced oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily for 14 days. Provided oral NSAIDs for analgesia and prostatic inflammation reduction. Midstream urine collected and sent for urgent culture and sensitivities.
Positive Behavioral Change: To encourage bladder emptying and reduce urinary stasis, the patient is advised to employ a scheduled voiding technique (attempting to urinate every 2 hours). Successfully emptying small amounts regularly will reduce the painful urgency and bladder distension, positively reinforcing the habit of frequent, low-pressure voiding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Donut cushion recommended for sitting to relieve direct perineal pressure.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to check urine culture sensitivities and confirm clinical response to antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if the patient becomes completely unable to pass urine, experiences worsening confusion, uncontrollable rigors, or persistent vomiting precluding oral antibiotic intake.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumed based on clinical findings; treatment may be adjusted depending on final urine culture results.
Clinical Course: Fevers and severe pain should begin to subside within 48 to 72 hours of antibiotic initiation. Prostate swelling will resolve more slowly over weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you cannot pass any urine for more than 4-6 hours, or if you begin vomiting and cannot keep your antibiotics down, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The prostate gland is acutely infected and swollen, causing your fevers and difficulty urinating. The prescribed antibiotics will target the bacteria, while the anti-inflammatories will reduce the swelling to help you pee.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics exactly as prescribed with food. Sit on a soft cushion to relieve pressure. Drink plenty of water and try to pee every two hours to keep the bladder clear.

